From 7cacf90765391c2bad5e4c1e8a2d08c90ce1a558 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 23:41:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Put Figure 1 on one shared radial scale, and add a
 difference row

Figure 1 normalised every response glyph to its own maximum, so all glyphs
came out the same size and only shape differed. For a healthy-vs-edema
comparison that hid the main effect. Now every glyph on the figure shares
ONE radial scale, so size carries meaning: a smaller glyph is a genuinely
smaller signal.

A third row holds the residual R_healthy(theta) - R_edema(theta). It is
exact rather than sampled -- the responses are zonal, so the difference is
the difference of their zonal coefficients. Radius is |difference| and
colour is the SIGNED difference, so where edema exceeds healthy reads as a
colour change instead of vanishing into a magnitude. Each title carries the
peak |difference| on the shared scale. The row is drawn only when there are
exactly two kernels, since a residual of three is not a thing.

A glyph is hard to read off a page, so the same numbers are printed:

      b      healthy      edema   difference
   0.005      0.9975     0.9834       0.0141
   1.000      0.8314     0.2915       0.5398
   2.000      0.7383     0.1555       0.5828
   3.000      0.6790     0.1159       0.5631

That table is the answer to why the edema arm will look noisier when the
noise is identical. It is not noisier: sigma = 1/SNR against S0 = 1 in both
runs. The anisotropic SIGNAL is 3 to 6 times smaller, because f 0.60 -> 0.10
removes most of the stick compartment, Deperp 0.50 -> 1.15 makes the
extra-axonal tensor nearly isotropic, and fw 0.02 -> 0.35 adds purely
isotropic signal. K_2, K_4 and K_6 all shrink while K_0 does not, and
deconvolution divides by K_l -- so the gain 1/K_l amplifies the same noise
more. Same mechanism as the CSF blow-ups already documented here. The
notebooks README now says this in place of leaving it to be rediscovered.

One claim written into the first draft of this change was wrong and the run
caught it. "At b = 0 the kernels are identical, so the residual is exactly
zero" is true only at exactly b = 0. This is a real protocol: its b = 0
volumes are b = 5 s/mm^2, all 18 of them, so they do attenuate slightly and
differently, and the measured residual there is 0.0141 rather than 0. The
text and the printed note now say that.

No simulation was run. The figure block was executed against the graphics
stubs with fabricated RUN data.

