Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Treat the b = 0 shell as exactly zero

The acquired .bval records EFFECTIVE b, so its 18 "b = 0" volumes are
b = 5 s/mm^2 -- the imaging gradients contribute a little diffusion
weighting of their own. Left as acquired that breaks the exact identity
S(0)/S0 = 1, makes the two kernels attenuate the lowest shell slightly
differently, and puts a small non-zero residual in Figure 1's b = 0 column,
which is a distraction rather than a result.

B0_SNAP in the Configuration block now sets any shell below 0.05 ms/um^2 to
exactly 0. Measured after the change:

  shells      b = [0 1 2 3], counts [18 90 90 90]  (binning check still passes)
  b = 0       healthy 1.0000, edema 1.0000, residual 0.000e+00

Only the b ~ 0 shell is snapped. The diffusion-weighted shells keep their
acquired jitter -- 990-1005, 1985-2010, 2980-3010 s/mm^2, 17 distinct values
-- so Step 1 still exercises SMI's shell binning on real numbers rather than
on a tidied protocol. Set B0_SNAP = 0 to use b exactly as acquired.

The tracked protocol file is unchanged and still holds the acquisition as
supplied; the snap is a visible, documented transformation in the notebook's
own configuration rather than an edit to the record of what was scanned.

Three dependent places updated to match: the Step 1 prose, the Step 4 check
(now S(b=0) == 1 to 1e-12 rather than within 1%, and it falls back to the
loose form if B0_SNAP is disabled), and Figure 1's note, where the b = 0
difference glyph is once again a point.

No simulation was run.

